center\">$\\bbox[5pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[5pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[5pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[5pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[5pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[5pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186203717 :question/worked-solution "Multiplication of integers in $\\mathbb Z_n$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\big[ \\, a \\, \\big] \\cdot \\big[ \\, b \\, \\big] = \\big[ \\, a \\cdot b \\, \\big]\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that we can pick any element of an equivalence class as its representative.\n\nWith that in mind, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[3] \\cdot [3] &= [9] = [6 + 3] = [3] \\\\\n[3] \\cdot [4] &= [12] = [2 \\cdot 6] = [0] \\\\\n[3] \\cdot [5] &= [15] = [2 \\cdot 6 + 3] = [3] \\\\\n[4] \\cdot [3] &= [12] = [2 \\cdot 6] = [0] \\\\\n[4] \\cdot [4] &= [16] = [2 \\cdot 6 + 4] = [4] \\\\\n[4] \\cdot [5] &= [20] = [3 \\cdot 6 + 2] = [2] \\\\\n[5] \\cdot [3] &= [15] = [2 \\cdot 6 + 3] = [3] \\\\\n[5] \\cdot [4] &= [20] = [3 \\cdot 6 + 2] = [2] \\\\\n[5] \\cdot [5] &= [25] = [4 \\cdot 6 + 1] = [1]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed multiplication table is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\times$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2$</td>\