ield-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$∠CAD≅∠AEG$</td>\n<td>{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$∠BAE≅∠AEG$</td>\n<td>{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$∠AEG≅∠BAE$</td>\n<td>Symmetric property of congruence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186204533 :question/problem "![](images/4c/4cbe42587db5bd7163680d6d80707ca65b7f7b89f71c4a1a8a04b9648b39c8d4.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the parallel lines $l$, $m$, and $n$ are crossed by the transversal $t$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $∠JHK≅∠AEG$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$∠JHK≅∠FEH$</td>\n<td>{{field-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$∠AEG≅∠FEH$</td>\n<td>{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$∠FEH≅∠AEG$</td>\n<td>Symmetric property of congruence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$∠JHK≅∠AEG$</td>\n<td>{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186204625 :question/problem "A magician has a $6$-sided die, and an audience member suspects it is biased towards landing on $1$. Let $X$ represent the number of times the die lands on $1$ when rolled a total of $5$ times. Under the null hypothesis that the die is fair, what is the critical region of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $5 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **probability mass function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X = x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4019$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4019$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1608$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0322$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0032$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0001$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186204626 :question/problem "Mary owns a pastry shop and believes her new muffin recipe will increase muffin sales. With the previous recipe, an average of $4$ out of $10$ customers bought at least one muffin. Let $X$ represent the number of customers buying at least one new muffin in a random sample of $8$. Under the null hypothesis that the proportion of customers that buy a muffin with the new recipe is the same as before, what is the critical region of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $2.5 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **cumulative distribution function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$⋯$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X \\le x)$</td>\n<td>$⋯$</td>\n<td>$0.5941$</td>\n<td>$0.8263$</td>\n<td>$0.9502$</td>\n<td>$0.9915$</td>\n<td>$0.9993$</td>\n<td>$1.0000$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186204627 :question/problem "A particular drug reduces symptoms of an illness for $75 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the patients in a trial. A new model of the drug is being tested, and the drug producer claims it is more effective than the previous model. Let $X$ be the number of patients in a random sample of $11$ that manifest a reduction of the symptoms after being treated with the new drug. Under the null hypothesis that the new model is as effective as the previous, what is the critical value of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $10 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **probability mass function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$⋯$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X = x)$</td>\n<td>$⋯$</td>\n<td>$0.0803$</td>\n<td>$0.1721$</td>\n<td>$0.2581$</td>\n<td>$0.2581$</td>\n<td>$0.1549$</td>\n<td>$0.0422$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186204628 :question/problem "The average number of monthly traffic incidents on a particular road is $2$. Some citizens think that removing a nearby semaphore will increase the number of incidents.\nLet $X$ represent the number of incidents on that road during a one-month period after removing the semaphore. Under the null hypothesis that the number of incidents is the same as before, what is the critical value of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $10 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **cumulative distribution function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$⋯$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$⋯$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X \\le x)$</td>\n<td>$⋯$</td>\n<td>$0.6767$</td>\n<td>$0.8571$</td>\n<td>$0.9473$</td>\n<td>$0.9834$</td>\n<td>$0.9955$</td>\n<td>$⋯$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186205081 :tutorial/content "Constructing a tape diagram for each configuration of a Turing machine can be cumbersome.\nFortunately, an **instantaneous description** (ID) provides a concise shorthand notation.\n\nFor a given configuration, the ID consists of the current sequence of symbols on the tape, with the current state placed just before the symbol being observed. For example, the ID of a machine with the configuration below is $1q_10$.\n\n![](images/12/12ea089d964a7f42c46155c57ae5ba9eca7916c4763bff2d5d062cb1fa3b8d70.png)\n\nTo a machine transitioning from one ID to another, we use the $\\vdash$ notation.\nFor example, if the machine above contains the command $(q_1,0) \\to (q_2, 1, R)$, the next transition is expressed as follows:\n$1q_10 \\:\\vdash\\: 11q_2\\square$\n\nIn this case, we add $\\square$ after $q_2$ because the head now observes a blank symbol. Let's now apply a Turing machine to a string using instantaneous descriptions.\n\nSuppose the string $00$ is written on the tape of the Turing machine given by the transition table below, where $\\square$ is the blank symbol and the tape head initially observes the left-most symbol of the string in state $q_1$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Large _{Q} \\!\\backslash\\! ^{\\Gamma}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\square$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\to \\! q_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_1,0,R)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_2,0,L)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_2,0,L)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(q_0,0,R)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\ast \\: q_0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe'll write a sequence of the machine's moves encoded using instantaneous descriptions.\nFirst, let's summarize the nontrivial commands from our transition table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Command</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Descriprion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\delta(q_1, 0) = (q_1, 0, R)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">In state $q_1$, observes $0{:}$<br>write $0$, stay in state $q_1$, and move right.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\delta(q_1, \\square) = (q_2, 0, L)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">In state $q_1$, observes $\\square{:}$<br>write $0$, swap to state $q_2$, and move left.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\delta(q_2, 0) = (q_2, 0, L)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">In state $q_2$, observes $0{:}$<br>write $0$, stay in state $q_2$, and move left.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\delta(q_2, \\square) = (q_0, 0, R)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">In state $q_2$, observes $\\square{:}$<br>write $0$, swap to state $q_0$, and move right.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, the machine goes through the following configurations:\n\n| Instantaneous Description | Tape Diagram |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $q_1 00$ | ![](images/1b/1be00c6c40e7c70eef5bb25cc8a8b281088036682a450022230d0deea409a0c4.png) |\n| $0 q_1 0$ | ![](images/59/5949ebabbfa32035e948c2c84b40d0a3d95d546c91bc2b35b12d3f2679258f7b.png) |\n| $00 q_1 \\square$ | ![](images/21/21787a9401b6343afe00950c2528437761a07d0f8fe93c85183fc19675cbcf42.png) |\n| $0 q_2 00$ | ![](images/56/563ce1442b18e5e68e978d7437f8f676c3f4ca1d25e823a44857448b2caec85c.png) |\n| $q_2 000$ | ![](images/8b/8b930074cd109b75f300a04715029fa24ea8638858ba56d28c0afe1335e6142b.png) |\n| $q_2 \\square000$ | ![](images/fc/fca031f24b6909f6a93b08539ba305af0ceb5e6577d65aa284948d2dc92c2e12.png) |\n| $0 q_0 000$ | ![](images/bc/bcc24b8e61b1958f2df46d5d1787bd982343df7b00ff0394dcc21d3cc1a721d8.png) |\n\nFinally, writing the sequence of IDs using $\\vdash$ notation, we get the following:\n\n$$\nq_{1}00 \\mid ⊢0q_{1}0; ⊢00q_{1}◻; ⊢0q_{2}00; ⊢q_{2}000; ⊢q_{2}◻000; ⊢0q_{0}000\n$$"] [17592186205096 :tutorial/content "![](images/5b/5b4351718878bc51859ebceb545d4a66b322e1f5383fe403cddca68d0fccf8fa.png)\n\nThe table below shows our proof of the converse alternate interior angles theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle WYS \\cong \\angle TYZ$</td>\n<td>Vertical angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle TYZ \\cong \\angle UZY$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle WYS \\cong \\angle UZY$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of congruence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$l$ and $m$ are parallel</td>\n<td>Converse corresponding angles postulate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now look at a proof of the converse alternate *exterior* angles theorem."] [17592186205098 :tutorial/content "![](images/e7/e71d2aa2239aa25bb6a2413b7114bca6abfb0f114a7fee6ee6d3489aa0d1cd36.png)\n\nWhen stating geometry proofs, we often present them in a so-called two-column format.\n\nThe table below shows the proof of the consecutive interior angles theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle{2}$ and $\\angle{3}$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>Linear pair postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{2} + m\\angle{3} = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle{2} \\cong \\angle{6}$</td>\n<td>Corresponding angles postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{2} = m\\angle{6}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{6} + m\\angle{3} = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\angle{3}$ and $\\angle{6}$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now consider a proof of the consecutive exterior angles theorem."] [17592186205100 :tutorial/content "![](images/a0/a0af160bd44faa187987613abd77fb7637fda781acfc4904cce0d9da1cb5e0fd.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof of the converse of the consecutive interior angles theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$m\\angle AQR = \\alpha$ and $m\\angle QRC = 180^\\circ - \\alpha$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle AQR$ and $\\angle AQP$ are supplementary angles</td>\n<td>Linear pair postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle AQR + m\\angle AQP = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle AQP = 180^\\circ - m\\angle AQR$</td>\n<td>Addition principle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle AQP = 180^\\circ - \\alpha$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle AQP = m\\angle QRC$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\angle AQP \\cong \\angle QRC$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$</td>\n<td>Converse corresponding angles postulate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, let's consider a proof of the converse consecutive *exterior* angles theorem."] [17592186205102 :tutorial/content "![](images/67/67f3dd53dfd7ef483b7c66cc79656090b26ebb7c633c847c2364a51ef476f670.png)\n\nThe table below shows our proof of the linear perpendicular theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle BAC \\cong \\angle CAD$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle BAC = m\\angle CAD$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>Linear pair postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle BAC + m\\angle CAD=180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle CAD + m\\angle CAD = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle CAD = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Solving for $m\\angle CAD$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$l$ and $m$ are perpendicular</td>\n<td>Definition of perpendicular lines</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186205104 :tutorial/content "![](images/9e/9e76e63832763d6378beedf3f21254993cf5ac56a25255d957e8c6d6fdef9a34.png)\n\nThe table below shows our proof of the perpendicular bisector theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$m\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ$, $m\\angle BCD = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of perpendicular lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle ACD =m\\angle BCD$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle ACD \\cong \\angle BCD$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{BC}$</td>\n<td>Definition of perpendicular bisector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$</td>\n<td>Reflexive property of congruence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{ACD} \\cong \\triangle{BCD}$</td>\n<td>SAS criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AD} \\cong \\overline{BD}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now consider an alternative proof that uses the Pythagorean theorem."] [17592186205107 :tutorial/content "![](images/8e/8ef6c221cc215037df9836cfedfb23193694e4770ec07e03ee6b3eca7c683eba.png)\n\nThe table below shows our proof of the converse perpendicular bisector theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AN} \\cong \\overline{BN}$, $\\overline{AM}\\cong \\overline{BM}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{MN} \\cong \\overline{MN}$</td>\n<td>Reflexive property of congruence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{AMN} \\cong \\triangle{BMN}$</td>\n<td>SSS criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle{MNA} \\cong \\angle{MNB}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{MN} \\perp \\overline{AB}$</td>\n<td>Linear pair perpendicular theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{MN}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$</td>\n<td>Definition of a perpendicular bisector</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can also prove this theorem using the HL criterion. Let's see an example."] [17592186205114 :tutorial/content "![](images/bf/bfa7b8059aef805fdfb8e3fe31cb1429a933a0b559c0b5465526591a806b2004.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof of the centroid ratio theorem in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AD}$ and $\\overline{BE}$ are medians of $\\triangle{ABC}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$D$ and $E$ are the midpoints of $\\overline{BC}$ and $\\overline{AC}$, respectively</td>\n<td>Definition of a median</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{DE}$ and $AB: DE = 2: 1$</td>\n<td>Midpoint theorem for triangles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ABG} \\cong \\angle{DEG}$, $\\angle{BAG} \\cong \\angle{EDG}$</td>\n<td>Alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{ABG} \\sim \\triangle{DEG}$</td>\n<td>AA similarity criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$AG: GD = 2: 1$</td>\n<td>CPSTP</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's look at a slightly different proof that uses the vertical angles theorem."] [17592186205120 :tutorial/content "![](images/14/146f01f2b906ce0a0b635a31f34ca466b56b5f69f409aa65028fa758808767e5.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof that parallelograms with congruent diagonals are rectangles in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$PQRS$ is a parallelogram</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{PQ} \\cong \\overline{RS}$</td>\n<td>Opposite sides are congruent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\overline{QS} \\cong \\overline{PR}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\overline{PS} \\cong \\overline{PS}$</td>\n<td>Reflexive property of congruence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{QPS} \\cong \\triangle{RSP}$</td>\n<td>SSS congruence criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\angle{QPS} \\cong \\angle{RSP}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{QPS} = m\\angle{RSP}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\angle{QPS}$ and $\\angle{RSP}$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>Adjacent angles are supplementary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{QPS} + m\\angle{RSP} = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">10</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{QPS} + m\\angle{QPS} = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">11</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{QPS} = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Algebra</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">12</td>\n<td>$\\angle{QPS}$ is a right angle</td>\n<td>Definition of a right angle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186205149 :tutorial/content "It will be convenient to collect all the relevant statistics for this problem in a **one-factor ANOVA table,** shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSB}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$K-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSB}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSB}}{K-1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSW}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-K$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSW}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{n-K}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SST}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nRecall that:\n\n- $\\text{SSW}$ is the sum of squares within groups,\n- $\\text{SSB}$ is the sum of squares between groups,\n- $\\text{SST}$ is the total sum of squares, and $\\text{SST}=\\text{SSW}+\\text{SSB}$,\n- $K$ is the number of groups,\n- $n$ is the total number of sample elements.\n\nFor example, suppose a particular feature is being examined across $5$ groups of individuals to determine if the mean varies by group.\nA sample of size $n=80$ is conducted and it is found that\n\n$$\n\\text{SSB} = 124, \\qquad \\text{SSW} = 262.5\n$$\n\nLet's complete the one-factor ANOVA table for this sample.\n\n- First, we are given $\\text{SSB}$ and $\\text{SSW}$, so we can compute $\\text{SST}$ as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle SST|= SSB + SSW; = 124 + 262.5; = 386.5\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSB}$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{B}|= K - 1; = 5 - 1; = 4.\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSW}$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{W}|= n - K; = 80 - 5; = 75.\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SST}$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{T}|= n - 1; = 80 - 1; = 79.\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares between groups is\n$$\n\\displaystyle MSB|= \\frac{SSB}{K - 1}; = \\frac{124}{5 - 1}; = 31.\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares within groups is\n$$\n\\displaystyle MSW|= \\frac{SSW}{n - K}; = \\frac{262.5}{80 - 5}; = 3.5.\n$$\n- Finally, the $F$ ratio is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}} = \\dfrac{31}{3.5} \\approx 8.857.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as follows.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$124$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8.857$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$262.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$386.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$79$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186205151 :tutorial/content "We've seen that each minterm has a value of $1$ for exactly one combination of its variables' values.\n\nAs a result, a minterm is in the principal disjunctive normal form of a Boolean function if and only if the binary tuple associated with the minterm evaluates the function to $1$.\n\nTo demonstrate this, let's find the PDNF of the Boolean function with $3$ variables, as defined by the following table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe PDNF of the function has $3$ minterms since this is the number of rows with output $1$ in the given truth table. The tuple in each of these rows corresponds to one of the minterms in the PCNF.\n\nThe rows containing ones of the function and their corresponding minterms are shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">$\\overline{x_1} \\land \\overline{x_2} \\land x_3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">$\\overline{x_1} \\land \\ x_2 \\land x_3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">$x_1 \\land \\overline{x_2} \\land x_3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nConsequently, the corresponding PDNF of the function $f$ is\n$f\\equiv (\\overline{x_1} \\land \\overline{x_2} \\land x_3)\\lor(\\overline{x_1} \\land \\ x_2 \\land x_3) \\lor (x_1 \\land \\overline{x_2} \\land x_3)$.\n\nThis method also works in reverse, i.e., we can construct the truth table of a function given in PDNF. Let's see how with an example."] [17592186205153 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the previous example in which we calculated the ANOVA table of a sample of $n=80$ data points distributed among $K=5$ groups:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$124$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8.857$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$262.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$386.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$79$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe want to test the following null and alternative hypotheses on the above data set:\n\n- $H_0{:}\\: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2 = \\mu_3= \\mu_4 = \\mu_5$\n- $H_1{:}$ There is at least one population mean that differs from the others,\n\nwhere $\\mu_1, \\mu_2, \\ldots, \\mu_5$ are the population means of each group.\n\nWe conduct the test as follows:\n\n- The $F$ ratio statistic test is\n$W = \\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}} \\approx 8.857$.\n- The numbers of degrees of freedom for this test are\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\nu_1 = df_B = 4, \\qquad \\nu_2 = df_W = 75.\n$$\n- Since this is a hypothesis test, we must choose an appropriate significance level. So, let's choose\n$\\alpha = 5\\%$.\n- Since we want to know whether the test statistic exceeds a certain threshold, the one factor ANOVA test is *always right-tailed*.\nUsing a Fisher's $F$-distribution table, the critical value for $\\nu_1=4$, $\\nu_2=75$ at a $5\\%$ significance level is $f_{\\textrm{critical}}=2.494$, and the critical region is\n$W\\geq 2.494$\nas shown below.![](images/51/5130b93235104686bf290e17443978985513d6b1013d89ef6f7837b1546e50b9.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(8.857)$ lies inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis.\n\nIn other words, the population means varies by group."] [17592186205156 :tutorial/content "We've seen that each maxterm has a value of $0$ for exactly one combination of its variables' values.\n\nAs a result, a maxterm is in the principal conjunctive normal form of a Boolean function if and only if the binary tuple associated with the maxterm evaluates the function to $0$.\n\nTo demonstrate this, consider the following principal conjunctive normal form (PCNF) of a Boolean function $f$.\n$f \\equiv (\\overline{x_1} \\lor x_2 \\lor \\overline{x_3}) \\land (\\overline{x_1} \\lor \\overline{x_2} \\lor x_3) \\land (\\overline{x_1} \\lor \\overline{x_2} \\lor \\overline{x_3})$\n\nLet's see how to fill the truth table for $f$ corresponding to the PCNF above.\n\nSince our PCNF has $3$ maxterms, the resulting truth table must contain $3$ rows with output $0$. The tuple in each of these rows corresponds to one of the maxterms in the PCNF.\n\nThe rows containing zeros of the function and their corresponding maxterms are shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">$\\overline{x_1} \\lor x_2 \\lor \\overline{x_3}$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\">$\\overline{x_1} \\lor \\overline{x_2} \\lor x_3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFE4C4\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\">$\\overline{x_1} \\lor \\overline{x_2} \\lor \\overline{x_3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis is the truth table corresponding to the Boolean function $f$.\n\nThis method also works in reverse, i.e., we can find the PCNF of a Boolean function defined by a given truth table. Let's see how with an example."]}
